/* NCERT Physics Class XII — Chapter 9: Ray Optics and Optical Instruments (book page 223–254)
   type 'num' = Numerical (step-by-step solution in sol); img = inline SVG graph/diagram */
addChapter({
  id: 'phy-12-09',
  subj: 'phy',
  cls: 12,
  no: 9,
  title: 'Ray Optics and Optical Instruments',
  src: 'NCERT Physics XII · Chapter 9',
  points: [
    { id: 'in1', topic: '9.1 Introduction', t: 'The eye perceives the world through light. Light is electromagnetic radiation of wavelength about 400 nm to 750 nm (the visible region). Light travels in straight lines in a homogeneous medium; a straight line along which light travels is a ray and a bundle of rays is a beam. Speed of light in vacuum c = 2.99792458 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹ (taken as 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹) — the highest speed attainable in nature.' },
    { id: 'in2', topic: 'Ray optics', t: 'Since the wavelength of light is very small compared to the size of ordinary objects (few cm or larger), a light wave can be considered to travel from one point to another along a straight line — the ray. This chapter treats reflection, refraction and dispersion using the ray picture and describes optical instruments such as microscopes and telescopes.' },
    { id: 'rf1', topic: '9.2 Reflection by spherical mirrors (Fig 9.1)', t: 'Laws of reflection: angle of reflection equals angle of incidence, and the incident ray, reflected ray and normal at the point of incidence lie in the same plane. For a curved mirror the normal is along the radius at that point. The geometric centre of a spherical mirror is its pole; the line through the pole and centre of curvature is the principal axis.' },
    { id: 'sc1', topic: '9.2.1 Cartesian sign convention (Fig 9.2)', t: 'All distances are measured from the pole of the mirror or optical centre of the lens. Distances measured in the direction of incident light are positive, those opposite are negative. Heights measured upwards (x-axis along principal axis) are positive, downwards negative. A single formula then serves all cases.' },
    { id: 'fm1', topic: '9.2.2 Focal length of spherical mirrors (Fig 9.3)', t: 'A parallel paraxial beam (rays close to and at small angles to the principal axis) converges to (concave) or appears to diverge from (convex) a point F on the axis — the principal focus. The distance from the pole to F is the focal length f. The plane through F normal to the axis is the focal plane. From geometry (small angles) f = R/2 (9.1).' },
    { id: 'me1', topic: '9.2.3 Mirror equation (Fig 9.4, 9.5)', t: 'If rays from a point actually converge at another point after reflection/refraction, it is a real image; if they only appear to diverge from it, a virtual image. Using two convenient rays and similar triangles for paraxial rays: 1/v + 1/u = 1/f (9.7) — the mirror equation, where u and v are object and image distances.' },
    { id: 'me2', topic: 'Linear magnification', t: 'Linear magnification m = h′/h = −v/u (9.8). Valid for concave and convex mirrors and real and virtual images. With sign convention, negative m means real and inverted image; positive m means virtual and erect.' },
    { id: 'me3', topic: 'Images by concave mirror (Fig 9.6)', t: 'For a concave mirror, an object between P and F gives a virtual, erect, magnified image behind the mirror; an object beyond F gives a real, inverted image. A convex mirror always gives a virtual, erect, diminished image between pole and focus (Fig 9.6(b)).' },
    { id: 'e901', topic: 'Example 9.1', t: 'Example 9.1: if the lower half of a concave mirror is covered with opaque material, the image is still of the whole object (laws of reflection hold for the remaining part), but its intensity is reduced (here to half).' },
    { id: 'e902', topic: 'Example 9.2 (Fig 9.7)', t: 'Example 9.2: a mobile phone lying along the principal axis of a concave mirror gives a distorted image — magnification is not uniform because different parts are at different distances. The part in the plane perpendicular to the axis (at C) forms its image in the same plane with the same size (B′C = BC). Distortion depends on location.' },
    { id: 'e903', topic: 'Example 9.3', t: 'Example 9.3: concave mirror R = 15 cm (f = −7.5 cm). (i) u = −10 cm: v = −30 cm, m = −3 — real, inverted, magnified. (ii) u = −5 cm: v = +15 cm (behind mirror), m = +3 — virtual, erect, magnified.' },
    { id: 'e904', topic: 'Example 9.4', t: 'Example 9.4: side-view convex mirror R = 2 m (f = 1 m); jogger approaching at 5 m s⁻¹. At 39 m, v = 39/40 m; after 1 s (u = −34) v = 34/35 m: image moves 1/280 m in 1 s. At 29 m, 19 m and 9 m the image speeds are 1/150, 1/60 and 1/10 m s⁻¹ — the image appears to speed up as the jogger comes closer.' },
    { id: 'rr1', topic: '9.3 Refraction (Fig 9.8)', t: 'When light crosses from one medium to another, part is reflected and part transmitted; the direction of propagation of the obliquely incident light changes at the interface — refraction. Snell\'s laws: (i) incident ray, refracted ray and normal lie in one plane; (ii) sin i/sin r = n₂₁ (9.10), the refractive index of medium 2 relative to medium 1 — a constant for a pair of media and a given wavelength.' },
    { id: 'rr2', topic: 'Optically denser and rarer', t: 'If n₂₁ > 1, r < i — the refracted ray bends towards the normal; medium 2 is optically denser. If n₂₁ < 1, it bends away from the normal (incidence in denser medium). Optical density is not mass density. n₂₁ = 1/n₁₂ (9.11) and n₃₂ = n₃₁ × n₁₂ (9.12).' },
    { id: 'rr3', topic: 'Lateral shift (Fig 9.9)', t: 'For a rectangular slab, refraction occurs at two interfaces; r₂ = i₁, so the emergent ray is parallel to the incident ray but shifted sideways — lateral shift.' },
    { id: 'rr4', topic: 'Apparent depth (Fig 9.10)', t: 'The bottom of a tank of water appears raised. Viewed near the normal, apparent depth h₁ = real depth h₂ divided by the refractive index of the medium (water) with respect to air. Rays from the bottom bend away from the normal on leaving water, so they appear to come from a raised point.' },
    { id: 'ti1', topic: '9.4 Total internal reflection (Fig 9.11)', t: 'When light goes from an optically denser to a rarer medium, it bends away from the normal and is partly reflected. As i increases, r reaches 90° at the critical angle i_c; for i > i_c no refraction occurs and all light is reflected back — total internal reflection (TIR). sin i_c = n₁₂ = 1/n₂₁ (9.14) for the rarer medium 1 being air.' },
    { id: 'ti2', topic: 'Table 9.1 — critical angles', t: 'Table 9.1 (refractive index, critical angle with air): water 1.33, 48.75°; crown glass 1.52, 41.14°; dense flint glass 1.62, 37.31°; diamond 2.42, 24.41°. A larger refractive index gives a smaller critical angle.' },
    { id: 'ti3', topic: 'Laser demonstration of TIR (Fig 9.12)', t: 'A laser beam through slightly turbid water (milk added) shows its path. Shone from below to strike the water surface, it partly reflects and partly refracts; striking more obliquely, refraction vanishes at a certain angle and the beam is totally reflected. In a long test tube, repeated TIR at the walls mimics an optical fibre. Never look into a laser beam or point it at anyone.' },
    { id: 'ti4', topic: '9.4.1 TIR prisms (Fig 9.13)', t: 'Prisms designed to bend light by 90° or 180° use total internal reflection; such a prism is also used to invert images without changing their size. For the first two, the critical angle of the prism material must be less than 45° — true for crown glass and dense flint glass.' },
    { id: 'ti5', topic: 'Optical fibres (Fig 9.14)', t: 'Optical fibres made of high-quality composite glass/quartz have a core of higher refractive index and a cladding of lower. Light entering at a suitable angle undergoes repeated TIR and emerges at the other end with no appreciable loss, even if the fibre is bent — an optical pipe. Used to transmit audio/video signals over long distances, as light pipes for visual examination of the esophagus, stomach and intestines (endoscopy), and in decorative fibre lamps.' },
    { id: 'ti6', topic: 'Low absorption in fibres', t: 'The main requirement is very little absorption over long distances, achieved by purification and special preparation of materials like quartz. In silica glass fibres more than 95% of the light can be transmitted over 1 km.' },
    { id: 'ss1', topic: '9.5.1 Refraction at a spherical surface (Fig 9.15)', t: 'For a spherical surface of radius R separating media of refractive indices n₁ (object side) and n₂, for paraxial rays: n₂/v − n₁/u = (n₂ − n₁)/R (9.16). This relation holds for any curved spherical surface with the sign convention.' },
    { id: 'e905', topic: 'Example 9.5', t: 'Example 9.5: a point source in air 100 cm from a spherical glass surface (n = 1.5, R = +20 cm): 1.5/v + 1/100 = 0.5/20 gives v = +100 cm — the image forms 100 cm from the surface in the direction of incident light.' },
    { id: 'ln1', topic: '9.5.2 Refraction by a lens (Fig 9.16)', t: 'Image formation by a thin lens: the first surface forms an image I₁ that acts as object for the second. Applying 9.16 twice: 1/f = (n₂₁ − 1)(1/R₁ − 1/R₂) (9.22) — the lensmaker\'s formula — and 1/v − 1/u = 1/f (9.23) — the thin lens formula. A lens has two foci F and F′ on either side.' },
    { id: 'ln2', topic: 'Convex and concave lenses (Fig 9.17)', t: 'For a double convex lens R₁ positive and R₂ negative, so f is positive — converging. For a double concave lens f is negative — diverging. Like mirrors, ray diagrams use three convenient rays: parallel to axis passes through (or appears from) the focus; ray through the optical centre goes undeviated; ray through the focus emerges parallel.' },
    { id: 'ln3', topic: 'Magnification by a lens', t: 'Linear magnification of a lens m = h′/h = v/u (9.24). For a concave lens and real object m is positive and less than 1 — virtual, erect, diminished image.' },
    { id: 'e906', topic: 'Example 9.6', t: 'Example 9.6: a magician makes a glass lens (n = 1.47) disappear in a trough of liquid: the liquid must have n = 1.47 so that n₂₁ = 1, 1/f = 0, f → ∞ — the lens acts like a plane sheet. The liquid is not water; it could be glycerine.' },
    { id: 'pw1', topic: '9.5.3 Power of a lens (Fig 9.18)', t: 'Power of a lens is a measure of its converging or diverging ability: P = tan δ/h; for small δ, P = 1/f (9.26). SI unit dioptre (D): 1 D = 1 m⁻¹ — the power of a lens of focal length 1 m. Power is positive for a converging lens and negative for a diverging lens: +2.5 D is a convex lens of focal length +40 cm; −4.0 D is a concave lens of focal length −25 cm.' },
    { id: 'e907', topic: 'Example 9.7', t: 'Example 9.7: (i) f = 0.5 m → P = +2 D. (ii) Double convex lens R₁ = 10 cm, R₂ = −15 cm, f = 12 cm gives n = 1.5. (iii) A convex lens of 20 cm focal length in air (n_glass = 1.5) has focal length +78.2 cm in water (n = 1.33).' },
    { id: 'cl1', topic: '9.5.4 Combination of thin lenses (Fig 9.19)', t: 'For two thin lenses A and B in contact, the image of the first is the object for the second; adding the lens equations: 1/f = 1/f₁ + 1/f₂ (9.29); for several lenses 1/f = 1/f₁ + 1/f₂ + 1/f₃ + … (9.30). In terms of power P = P₁ + P₂ + P₃ + … (9.31). Total magnification m = m₁m₂m₃… (9.32). Used in cameras, microscopes and telescopes; power of a combination of lenses is designed by opticians.' },
    { id: 'e908', topic: 'Example 9.8 (Fig 9.20)', t: 'Example 9.8: three lenses (f₁ = +10 cm, f₂ = −10 cm, f₃ = +30 cm, spaced 5 cm and 10 cm apart) with object 30 cm before the first: v₁ = 15 cm; virtual object 10 cm right of the second gives v₂ = ∞; the third lens then gives v₃ = 30 cm — the final image is 30 cm to the right of the third lens.' },
    { id: 'pr1', topic: '9.6 Refraction through a prism (Fig 9.21)', t: 'A ray passes through a triangular prism ABC: incidence i and refraction r₁ at face AB, internal incidence r₂ and emergence e at face AC. Since the quadrilateral AQNR has two right angles, r₁ + r₂ = A (9.34). Deviation δ = (i − r₁) + (e − r₂) = i + e − A (9.35).' },
    { id: 'pr2', topic: 'Deviation versus incidence (Fig 9.22)', t: 'The δ–i plot shows that any value of δ (except the minimum) corresponds to two values of i (and e), by the symmetry of i and e — the path can be traced back. At minimum deviation D_m, the refracted ray inside the prism is parallel to the base: i = e and r₁ = r₂ = A/2 (9.36), and i = (A + D_m)/2 (9.37).' },
    { id: 'pr3', topic: 'Refractive index from prism', t: 'n₂₁ = sin[(A + D_m)/2] / sin(A/2) (9.38): measuring A and D_m gives the refractive index of the prism material. For a thin prism (small A), D_m = (n₂₁ − 1)A — thin prisms do not deviate light much.' },
    { id: 'oi1', topic: '9.7 Optical instruments', t: 'Many optical devices — periscopes, kaleidoscopes, binoculars, telescopes, microscopes — use reflecting and refracting properties of mirrors, lenses and prisms. The human eye is one of the most important optical devices nature has endowed us with.' },
    { id: 'sm1', topic: '9.7.1 Simple microscope (Fig 9.23)', t: 'A simple magnifier or microscope is a converging lens of small focal length held near the eye with the object inside f, giving an erect, magnified, virtual image. The closest distance for comfortable viewing is the near point D ≈ 25 cm. With the image at the near point, linear magnification m = 1 + D/f (9.39).' },
    { id: 'sm2', topic: 'Angular magnification of a simple microscope', t: 'Magnifying power is the ratio of the angle subtended by the image (at the eye) to that subtended by the object placed at the near point without the lens: m = θ/θ₀. For the image at the near point m = 1 + D/f; for the image at infinity (relaxed eye) m = D/f (9.43). For f = 5 cm, m ≈ 5–6.' },
    { id: 'cm1', topic: 'Compound microscope (Fig 9.24)', t: 'A compound microscope uses an objective (short focal length f₀) forming a real, inverted, magnified image near the first focal point of the eyepiece, which acts as a simple microscope giving the final virtual, magnified image at infinity or the near point. The distance L between the second focal point of the objective and the first focal point of the eyepiece is the tube length.' },
    { id: 'cm2', topic: 'Magnifying power of compound microscope', t: 'Magnification by objective m₀ = h′/h = L/f₀ (9.44). For final image at infinity, eyepiece gives m_e = D/f_e; total m = m₀m_e = (L/f₀)(D/f_e) (9.45). With f₀ = 1.0 cm, f_e = 2.0 cm and L = 20 cm, m = 250. Both f₀ and f_e should be small; in practice f much smaller than 1 cm is difficult; multi-component lenses reduce aberrations.' },
    { id: 'ts1', topic: '9.7.2 Refracting telescope (Fig 9.25)', t: 'A telescope gives angular magnification of distant objects. Its objective has a large focal length and much larger aperture than the eyepiece. A real image forms at the second focal point of the objective; the eyepiece magnifies it giving a final inverted image. Magnifying power m = β/α = f₀/f_e (9.46); tube length f₀ + f_e (normal adjustment). Terrestrial telescopes have an extra pair of inverting lenses.' },
    { id: 'ts2', topic: 'Example and light gathering power', t: 'With f₀ = 100 cm and f_e = 1 cm, m = 100: two stars 1′ apart appear 100′ = 1.67° apart. Main considerations for an astronomical telescope are light-gathering power (depends on objective area — fainter objects with larger diameters) and resolving power (ability to see two objects in nearly the same direction separately), which also depends on the objective diameter.' },
    { id: 'ts3', topic: 'Limitations of large lenses', t: 'The largest lens objective in use (40 inch, ~1.02 m) is at the Yerkes Observatory, Wisconsin, USA. Big lenses are very heavy, hard to support by their edges, difficult and expensive to make free of chromatic aberration and distortions. So modern telescopes use a concave mirror objective — reflecting telescopes.' },
    { id: 'ts4', topic: 'Reflecting telescopes (Fig 9.26)', t: 'Mirrors have no chromatic aberration and weigh less than lenses of equivalent optical quality, supported over the whole back surface. The objective focusses light inside the tube: in the 200-inch (~5.08 m) Mt. Palomar telescope the observer sits in a cage near the focal point. The Cassegrain telescope uses a convex secondary mirror to send light through a hole in the primary — a large focal length in a short telescope.' },
    { id: 'ts5', topic: 'Large telescopes', t: 'The largest telescope in India is at Kavalur, Tamil Nadu — a 2.34 m diameter Cassegrain reflector, used by the Indian Institute of Astrophysics, Bangalore. The largest reflecting telescopes in the world are the pair of Keck telescopes in Hawaii, USA, with 10 m reflectors.' },
    { id: 'su1', topic: 'Summary (reflection, refraction)', t: 'Reflection: ∠i = ∠r′; refraction: sin i/sin r = n, with rays and normal coplanar. Critical angle i_c: angle of refraction 90°; for i > i_c TIR — diamond (i_c ≈ 24.4°), totally reflecting prisms and mirage; optical fibres (glass fibres coated with lower-index material) carry light even when bent. Cartesian sign convention. Mirror equation 1/v + 1/u = 1/f, f ≈ R/2; f negative for concave, positive for convex.' },
    { id: 'su2', topic: 'Summary (lenses, prism, instruments)', t: 'Prism n₂₁ = sin[(A + D_m)/2]/sin(A/2). Spherical surface n₂/v − n₁/u = (n₂ − n₁)/R. Thin lens 1/v − 1/u = 1/f; lensmaker 1/f = [(n₂ − n₁)/n₁](1/R₁ − 1/R₂); f positive for converging. P = 1/f in dioptres; combination 1/f = Σ1/fᵢ, P = ΣPᵢ. Dispersion is splitting of light into constituent colours. Simple microscope m = 1 + D/f (D/f at infinity); compound m = m_e × m₀ ≈ (L/f₀)(D/f_e); telescope m = β/α = f₀/f_e.' },
    { id: 'pp1', topic: 'Points to ponder: images', t: 'Laws of reflection and refraction hold for all surfaces at the point of incidence. A real image exists in space even without a screen — the screen just diffuses the converging-diverging rays to our eye (seen in laser shows). Image formation needs regular reflection/refraction — all rays from a point must reach the same image point, which is why a page of a book gives no image.' },
    { id: 'pp2', topic: 'Points to ponder: colours and microscope', t: 'Thick lenses give coloured images due to dispersion. The colours of objects depend on the constituent colours of the incident light; monochromatic light can give an entirely different perception. A simple microscope\'s angular size of image equals that of object, yet it magnifies because we can keep the object much closer than 25 cm, subtending a large angle, while the image is at 25 cm.' },
    { id: 'xe1', topic: 'Exercises 9.1–9.10', t: 'Exercises: candle 2.5 cm at 27 cm before a concave mirror R = 36 cm (screen at 54 cm, image 5 cm, real inverted); needle 4.5 cm at 12 cm from a convex mirror f = 15 cm (image 6.7 cm behind, 2.5 cm); apparent depth 9.4 cm of 12.5 cm water (n = 1.33; with n = 1.63 microscope moves 1.7 cm); refraction water to glass; bulb 80 cm deep in water (area ≈ 2.6 m²); prism A = 60°, D_m = 40° (n ≈ 1.53; in water D_m ≈ 10°); lens radius for f = 20 cm, n = 1.55 (22 cm); converging beam and lenses; concave lens image; convex 30 cm with concave 20 cm (f = −60 cm, diverging).' },
    { id: 'xe2', topic: 'Exercises 9.11–9.20', t: 'Exercises: compound microscopes (f₀ = 2.0 cm, f_e = 6.25 cm, 15 cm apart: object 2.5 cm, m = 20 for near point; 2.59 cm, m = 13.5 at infinity; f₀ = 8.0 mm, f_e = 2.5 cm: separation 9.47 cm, m = 88); telescopes (144 cm/6.0 cm: m = 24, length 150 cm; 15 m/1.0 cm: m = 1500, moon image 13.7 cm); mirror equation deductions; pin seen through a 15 cm glass slab (raised 5 cm); light pipe n = 1.68 with cladding 1.44; maximum focal length 0.75 m to image on a wall 3 m away; displacement method f = 21.4 cm; two-lens system 8 cm apart.' },
    { id: 'xe3', topic: 'Exercises 9.21–9.31', t: 'Exercises: incidence 29.75° for just TIR in a 60° prism (n = 1.524); magnifying glass and squares (f = 9 cm: object at 6.62 cm, m = 3.8; object 5.4 cm for 6.25 mm² image); eye position and short focal lengths; microscope set-up for 30X (f₀ = 1.25 cm, f_e = 5 cm: object 1.5 cm, separation 11.67 cm); telescope 140 cm/5.0 cm (m = 28 normal, 33.6 at near point; tower image 4.7 cm); Cassegrain mirrors (image 315 mm from the secondary); galvanometer mirror deflection 3.5° (spot shifts 18.4 cm at 1.5 m); liquid under equiconvex lens (n ≈ 1.33).' }
  ],
  qs: [
    { id: 'q1', p: ['in1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The visible region of light has wavelengths about:', o: ['10 nm to 400 nm', '400 nm to 750 nm', '750 nm to 1 mm', '1 mm to 1 m'], a: 1, e: '9.1.' },
    { id: 'q2', p: ['in1'], type: 'st', lvl: 1, st: ['A bundle of rays is called a beam.', 'Speed of light in vacuum is the highest speed attainable in nature.'], a: 0, e: '9.1.' },
    { id: 'q3', p: ['in2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Light can be treated as travelling along straight rays in ray optics.', R: 'The wavelength of light is very small compared to the size of ordinary objects.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q4', p: ['rf1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'For a spherical mirror, the normal at the point of incidence is along:', o: ['The principal axis', 'The tangent', 'The radius of curvature at that point', 'The focal plane'], a: 2, e: '9.2.' },
    { id: 'q5', p: ['rf1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['The incident ray, reflected ray and normal lie in the same plane.', 'The angle of reflection is twice the angle of incidence.'], a: 2, e: 'They are equal.' },
    { id: 'q6', p: ['sc1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 300 140" width="300" height="140" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><line x1="10" y1="80" x2="290" y2="80" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><path d="M 150 15 A 120 120 0 0 1 150 145" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2.4"/><line x1="150" y1="80" x2="280" y2="80" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="280,80 274.6,82.6 274.6,77.4" fill="#D9A520"/><text x="270" y="72" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">+ (along incident light)</text><line x1="150" y1="80" x2="20" y2="80" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="20,80 25.4,77.4 25.4,82.6" fill="currentColor"/><text x="25" y="72" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">−</text><line x1="150" y1="80" x2="150" y2="20" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="150,20 152.6,25.4 147.4,25.4" fill="currentColor"/><text x="156" y="26" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">+ up</text><line x1="40" y1="40" x2="130" y2="40" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="130,40 124.6,42.6 124.6,37.4" fill="currentColor"/><text x="60" y="32" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">incident light</text></svg>', q: 'In the Cartesian sign convention, distances measured in the direction of the incident light are:', o: ['Negative', 'Always zero', 'Positive only for virtual images', 'Positive'], a: 3, e: '9.2.1.' },
    { id: 'q7', p: ['sc1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Heights measured downwards from the axis are positive.', 'All distances are measured from the pole or optical centre.'], a: 3, e: 'Downwards are negative.' },
    { id: 'q8', p: ['fm1'], type: 'num', lvl: 1, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 300 170" width="300" height="170" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><line x1="10" y1="90" x2="290" y2="90" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><path d="M 250 15 A 160 160 0 0 1 250 165" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2.4"/><circle cx="253" cy="90" r="2" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="262" y="104" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">P</text><circle cx="173" cy="90" r="3" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="173" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">F</text><circle cx="93" cy="90" r="3" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="93" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">C</text><line x1="40" y1="50" x2="245" y2="50" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="245,50 239.6,52.6 239.6,47.4" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="245" y1="50" x2="173" y2="90" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="40" y1="130" x2="245" y2="130" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="245,130 239.6,132.6 239.6,127.4" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="245" y1="130" x2="173" y2="90" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="213" y="160" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">f = R/2</text></svg>', q: 'A concave mirror has radius of curvature 40 cm. Its focal length is:', o: ['−40 cm', '−20 cm', '+20 cm', '−80 cm'], a: 1, e: 'f = R/2, negative for concave.', sol: ['f = R/2', '= 40/2', '= 20 cm (concave)', 'Answer: −20 cm'] },
    { id: 'q9', p: ['fm1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'The relation f = R/2 holds for paraxial rays.', R: 'For paraxial rays the angles involved are small.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q10', p: ['me1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The mirror equation is:', o: ['1/v + 1/u = 1/f', '1/v − 1/u = 1/f', 'v + u = f', '1/u − 1/v = 1/f'], a: 0, e: 'Eq. 9.7.' },
    { id: 'q11', p: ['me1', 'xe1'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'A candle is 27 cm in front of a concave mirror of radius 36 cm. The image distance is:', o: ['−10.8 cm', '+54 cm', '−54 cm', '−27 cm'], a: 2, e: 'Exercise 9.1.', sol: ['f = −18 cm, u = −27 cm', '1/v = 1/f − 1/u = −1/18 + 1/27', '= −1/54', 'Answer: v = −54 cm (real)'] },
    { id: 'q12', p: ['me2', 'xe1'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'For the candle (2.5 cm) of the previous question, the image height is:', o: ['+5 cm', '−1.25 cm', '−2.5 cm', '−5 cm'], a: 3, e: 'm = −v/u.', sol: ['m = −v/u = −(−54)/(−27)', '= −2', 'h′ = −2 × 2.5', 'Answer: −5 cm (inverted, magnified)'] },
    { id: 'q13', p: ['me2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['A negative magnification means a real and inverted image.', 'Magnification m = +v/u for mirrors.'], a: 2, e: 'm = −v/u.' },
    { id: 'q14', p: ['me3', 'xe1'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'A 4.5 cm needle is 12 cm from a convex mirror of focal length 15 cm. The image distance is about:', o: ['+6.7 cm', '−6.7 cm', '+60 cm', '+27 cm'], a: 0, e: 'Exercise 9.2.', sol: ['f = +15, u = −12', '1/v = 1/15 + 1/12 = 9/60', 'v = +6.67 cm', 'Answer: 6.7 cm behind the mirror (virtual)'] },
    { id: 'q15', p: ['me3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'A convex mirror always forms an image that is:', o: ['Real and inverted', 'Virtual, erect and diminished', 'Virtual and magnified', 'Real and diminished'], a: 1, e: '9.2.3.' },
    { id: 'q16', p: ['me3'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['A convex mirror can give a real image of a real object.', 'A concave mirror gives a virtual, erect, magnified image of an object between P and F.'], a: 3, e: 'Always virtual.' },
    { id: 'q17', p: ['e901'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'If the lower half of a concave mirror is blackened, the image of an object:', o: ['Shows only the upper half', 'Shows only the lower half', 'Is complete but less bright', 'Disappears'], a: 2, e: 'Example 9.1.' },
    { id: 'q18', p: ['e901'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Covering half of a mirror does not cut off half of the image.', R: 'Every part of the mirror forms the complete image.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q19', p: ['e902'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['The image of a phone lying along the axis of a concave mirror is distorted.', 'Different parts of the phone are at the same distance from the mirror.'], a: 2, e: 'Different distances.' },
    { id: 'q20', p: ['e903'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'An object is 10 cm in front of a concave mirror of radius 15 cm. The magnification is:', o: ['+3', '−3', '−1.5', '+1.5'], a: 1, e: 'Example 9.3(i).', sol: ['f = −7.5 cm, u = −10 cm', '1/v = −1/7.5 + 1/10 → v = −30 cm', 'm = −v/u = −(−30)/(−10)', 'Answer: −3 (real, inverted)'] },
    { id: 'q21', p: ['e903'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'The same object at 5 cm from the mirror gives an image at:', o: ['15 cm in front', '30 cm in front', '15 cm behind the mirror', '3 cm behind'], a: 2, e: 'Example 9.3(ii).', sol: ['u = −5 cm, f = −7.5 cm', '1/v = −1/7.5 + 1/5 = 1/15', 'v = +15 cm', 'Answer: virtual, erect, m = +3'] },
    { id: 'q22', p: ['e904'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'A convex side mirror (f = 1 m); a jogger at 39 m. The image distance is:', o: ['40/39 m', '1 m', '39 m', '39/40 m behind the mirror'], a: 3, e: 'Example 9.4.', sol: ['v = fu/(u − f)', '= 1 × (−39)/(−39 − 1)', '= 39/40 m', 'Answer: 0.975 m'] },
    { id: 'q23', p: ['e904'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'The image of an approaching jogger in a convex mirror appears to speed up.', R: 'The image distance changes faster as the object comes closer to the mirror.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q24', p: ['rr1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Snell\'s law states:', o: ['sin r/sin i = n₂₁', 'i/r = n₂₁', 'cos i/cos r = n₂₁', 'sin i/sin r = n₂₁'], a: 3, e: 'Eq. 9.10.' },
    { id: 'q25', p: ['rr1'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'Light enters glass (n = √3) from air at 60°. The angle of refraction is:', o: ['30°', '45°', '60°', '35°'], a: 0, e: 'Snell\'s law.', sol: ['sin r = sin 60°/√3', '= (√3/2)/√3', '= 1/2', 'Answer: 30°'] },
    { id: 'q26', p: ['rr2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Optical density is the same as mass density.', 'A ray going into an optically denser medium bends towards the normal.'], a: 3, e: 'Optical density is not mass density.' },
    { id: 'q27', p: ['rr2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'n of glass w.r.t. air is 1.5 and of water w.r.t. air is 1.33. The refractive index of glass w.r.t. water is about:', o: ['0.89', '1.13', '2.0', '1.99'], a: 1, e: 'n_gw = n_ga/n_wa.', sol: ['n_gw = n_ga × n_aw', '= 1.5/1.33', '= 1.128', 'Answer: ≈ 1.13'] },
    { id: 'q28', p: ['rr3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 260 160" width="260" height="160" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><rect x="30" y="55" width="200" height="50" fill="#D9A520" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" fill-opacity="0.15"/><line x1="60" y1="10" x2="100" y2="55" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="100,55 94.5,52.7 98.4,49.2" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="100" y1="55" x2="130" y2="105" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2"/><line x1="130" y1="105" x2="170" y2="150" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="170,150 164.5,147.7 168.4,144.2" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="100" y1="55" x2="140" y2="100" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/><line x1="140" y1="100" x2="165" y2="128" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/><text x="200" y="140" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">lateral shift</text></svg>', q: 'For a ray through a rectangular glass slab, the emergent ray is:', o: ['Parallel to the incident ray but laterally shifted', 'Perpendicular to the incident ray', 'Along the normal', 'Totally reflected'], a: 0, e: '9.3.' },
    { id: 'q29', p: ['rr4', 'xe1'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 260 160" width="260" height="160" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><rect x="30" y="40" width="200" height="100" fill="#D9A520" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" fill-opacity="0.15"/><circle cx="130" cy="135" r="4" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="140" y="150" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">O (real)</text><circle cx="130" cy="105" r="3" fill="#D9A520" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="140" y="108" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">I (apparent)</text><line x1="130" y1="135" x2="160" y2="40" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="160" y1="40" x2="185" y2="8" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="185,8 183.7,13.9 179.6,10.7" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="160" y1="40" x2="130" y2="105" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/><text x="30" y="34" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">air / water</text><text x="225" y="100" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">h₂</text></svg>', q: 'Water 12.5 cm deep; the needle at the bottom appears 9.4 cm deep. The refractive index of water is:', o: ['1.50', '0.75', '1.33', '1.25'], a: 2, e: 'Exercise 9.3.', sol: ['n = real depth/apparent depth', '= 12.5/9.4', '= 1.33', 'Answer: 1.33'] },
    { id: 'q30', p: ['rr4', 'xe2'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'A pin is viewed through a 15 cm thick glass slab (n = 1.5). It appears raised by:', o: ['10 cm', '7.5 cm', '15 cm', '5 cm'], a: 3, e: 'Exercise 9.16.', sol: ['Shift = t(1 − 1/n)', '= 15(1 − 2/3)', '= 5', 'Answer: 5 cm (independent of slab location)'] },
    { id: 'q31', p: ['rr4'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'The bottom of a water tank appears raised.', R: 'Light from the bottom bends away from the normal on entering air.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q32', p: ['ti1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 300 150" width="300" height="150" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><line x1="10" y1="60" x2="290" y2="60" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"/><rect x="10" y="60" width="280" height="85" fill="#D9A520" stroke="none" stroke-width="1.4" fill-opacity="0.12"/><text x="20" y="52" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">rarer (air)</text><text x="20" y="140" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">denser</text><circle cx="150" cy="135" r="4" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="150" y1="135" x2="110" y2="60" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="110" y1="60" x2="70" y2="15" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="70,15 75.5,17.3 71.6,20.8" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="150" y1="135" x2="85.9" y2="60" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="86.2" y1="60" x2="10" y2="60" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="10,60 15.4,57.4 15.4,62.6" fill="#D9A520"/><line x1="150" y1="135" x2="240" y2="60" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="240" y1="60" x2="290" y2="102" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="290,102 284.2,100.5 287.5,96.5" fill="#D9A520"/><text x="270" y="92" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">TIR</text><text x="30" y="75" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">i = i_c, r = 90°</text></svg>', q: 'Total internal reflection occurs when light goes from:', o: ['Rarer to denser medium', 'Denser to rarer medium at angle greater than critical angle', 'Denser to rarer at any angle', 'Rarer to denser at critical angle'], a: 1, e: '9.4.' },
    { id: 'q33', p: ['ti1'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'The critical angle for a medium is 30°. Its refractive index is:', o: ['2', '1.5', '√2', '√3'], a: 0, e: 'sin i_c = 1/n.', sol: ['n = 1/sin i_c', '= 1/sin 30°', '= 1/0.5', 'Answer: 2'] },
    { id: 'q34', p: ['ti1', 'xe1'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'A bulb is 80 cm deep in water (n = 1.33). The area of the water surface through which light emerges is about:', o: ['0.91 m²', '2.6 m²', '1.3 m²', '5.2 m²'], a: 1, e: 'Exercise 9.5.', sol: ['i_c = sin⁻¹(1/1.33) = 48.75°', 'r = 0.8 tan 48.75° = 0.91 m', 'Area = πr² = π × 0.83', 'Answer: ≈ 2.6 m²'] },
    { id: 'q35', p: ['ti2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'From Table 9.1, the substance with the smallest critical angle is:', o: ['Water', 'Crown glass', 'Diamond', 'Dense flint glass'], a: 2, e: 'Diamond 24.41°.' },
    { id: 'q36', p: ['ti2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Diamond sparkles brilliantly.', R: 'Its small critical angle (≈ 24.4°) causes multiple total internal reflections.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q37', p: ['ti3'], type: 'st', lvl: 1, st: ['Adding a few drops of milk to water makes a laser beam\'s path visible.', 'It is safe to look directly into a laser beam.'], a: 2, e: 'Never look into a laser.' },
    { id: 'q38', p: ['ti4'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Prisms that bend light by 90° or 180° using TIR need a material with critical angle:', o: ['More than 45°', 'Exactly 90°', 'Exactly 60°', 'Less than 45°'], a: 3, e: '9.4.1.' },
    { id: 'q39', p: ['ti4'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Crown glass cannot be used for totally reflecting prisms.', 'A totally reflecting prism can invert an image without changing its size.'], a: 3, e: 'Its critical angle 41° < 45°, so it can.' },
    { id: 'q40', p: ['ti5'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 300 100" width="300" height="100" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><rect x="20" y="30" width="260" height="40" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><rect x="20" y="38" width="260" height="24" fill="#D9A520" stroke="none" stroke-width="1.4" fill-opacity="0.15"/><polyline points="10,50 40,38 80,62 120,38 160,62 200,38 240,62 280,40" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2" stroke-linejoin="round"/><text x="150" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">core (higher n), cladding (lower n)</text><text x="150" y="22" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">repeated total internal reflection</text></svg>', q: 'In an optical fibre, the refractive index of the core is:', o: ['Higher than that of the cladding', 'Lower than that of the cladding', 'Equal to that of the cladding', 'Equal to 1'], a: 0, e: '9.4.1.' },
    { id: 'q41', p: ['ti5'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 300 100" width="300" height="100" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><rect x="20" y="30" width="260" height="40" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><rect x="20" y="38" width="260" height="24" fill="#D9A520" stroke="none" stroke-width="1.4" fill-opacity="0.15"/><polyline points="10,50 40,38 80,62 120,38 160,62 200,38 240,62 280,40" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2" stroke-linejoin="round"/><text x="150" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">core (higher n), cladding (lower n)</text><text x="150" y="22" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">repeated total internal reflection</text></svg>', ar: { A: 'Light travels along a bent optical fibre.', R: 'It undergoes repeated total internal reflections.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q42', p: ['ti5', 'xe2'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'A light pipe has core n = 1.68 and cladding n = 1.44. The critical angle at the core–cladding interface is about:', o: ['36.5°', '41°', '59°', '31°'], a: 2, e: 'Exercise 9.17.', sol: ['sin i_c = 1.44/1.68', '= 0.857', 'i_c ≈ 59°', 'Answer: ≈ 59° (TIR for incidence 0°–60° at the end)'] },
    { id: 'q43', p: ['ti6'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Silica fibres can transmit more than 95% of light over 1 km.', 'Optical fibres are used in endoscopy to view internal organs.'], a: 0, e: '9.4.1.' },
    { id: 'q44', p: ['ss1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Refraction at a spherical surface is governed by:', o: ['n₁/v − n₂/u = (n₂ − n₁)/R', 'n₂/v − n₁/u = (n₂ − n₁)/R', '1/v − 1/u = 1/f', 'n₂/v + n₁/u = R'], a: 1, e: 'Eq. 9.16.' },
    { id: 'q45', p: ['e905'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'A point source in air is 100 cm from a convex glass surface (n = 1.5, R = 20 cm). The image is at:', o: ['50 cm inside', '100 cm in air', '60 cm inside', '100 cm inside the glass'], a: 3, e: 'Example 9.5.', sol: ['1.5/v − 1/(−100) = 0.5/20', '1.5/v = 0.025 − 0.01 = 0.015', 'v = 100 cm', 'Answer: +100 cm'] },
    { id: 'q46', p: ['ln1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The lensmaker\'s formula is:', o: ['1/f = (n₂₁ + 1)(1/R₁ + 1/R₂)', 'f = (n − 1)R', '1/f = (n₂₁ − 1)(1/R₁ − 1/R₂)', '1/f = 1/v + 1/u'], a: 2, e: 'Eq. 9.22.' },
    { id: 'q47', p: ['ln1', 'xe1'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'Double-convex lenses (n = 1.55) with equal radii must have f = 20 cm. The radius needed is:', o: ['22 cm', '11 cm', '20 cm', '40 cm'], a: 0, e: 'Exercise 9.7.', sol: ['1/f = (n − 1)(2/R)', '1/20 = 0.55 × 2/R', 'R = 1.1 × 20', 'Answer: 22 cm'] },
    { id: 'q48', p: ['ln1'], type: 'num', lvl: 1, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 300 170" width="300" height="170" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><line x1="10" y1="90" x2="290" y2="90" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><path d="M 150 15 Q 175 90 150 165 Q 125 90 150 15" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2"/><circle cx="90" cy="90" r="3" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="90" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">F</text><circle cx="210" cy="90" r="3" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="210" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">F′</text><line x1="40" y1="90" x2="40" y2="50" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.4"/><line x1="40" y1="50" x2="150" y2="50" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="150,50 145.5,52.2 145.5,47.8" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="150" y1="50" x2="280" y2="147" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="40" y1="50" x2="280" y2="130" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="40" y="40" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">object</text><text x="240" y="160" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">real inverted image</text></svg>', q: 'An object is 30 cm from a convex lens of focal length 20 cm. The image is at:', o: ['12 cm', '60 cm on the other side', '−60 cm', '50 cm'], a: 1, e: '1/v − 1/u = 1/f.', sol: ['1/v = 1/f + 1/u', '= 1/20 − 1/30', '= 1/60', 'Answer: v = +60 cm'] },
    { id: 'q49', p: ['ln2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'A double concave lens is diverging.', R: 'Its focal length is negative.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q50', p: ['ln2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['A ray through the optical centre of a thin lens goes undeviated.', 'For a double convex lens, R₁ is negative and R₂ positive.'], a: 2, e: 'R₁ positive, R₂ negative.' },
    { id: 'q51', p: ['ln3', 'xe1'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'A 3.0 cm object is 14 cm in front of a concave lens of focal length 21 cm. The image is:', o: ['42 cm, real, 9 cm', '8.4 cm, real, 1.8 cm', '8.4 cm from lens, virtual, 1.8 cm', '21 cm, virtual, 4.5 cm'], a: 2, e: 'Exercise 9.9.', sol: ['1/v = −1/21 + 1/(−14) = −5/42', 'v = −8.4 cm', 'm = v/u = 0.6', 'Answer: virtual, erect, 1.8 cm'] },
    { id: 'q52', p: ['ln3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'A concave lens always forms (for a real object) an image that is:', o: ['Real and inverted', 'Virtual and magnified', 'Real and magnified', 'Virtual, erect and diminished'], a: 3, e: '9.5.2.' },
    { id: 'q53', p: ['e906'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'A glass lens (n = 1.47) becomes invisible in a liquid. The liquid\'s refractive index is:', o: ['1.47', '1.33', '1.00', '2.94'], a: 0, e: 'Example 9.6.' },
    { id: 'q54', p: ['e906'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'A lens immersed in a liquid of the same refractive index behaves like a plane sheet.', R: 'Its focal length becomes infinite.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q55', p: ['pw1'], type: 'num', lvl: 1, q: 'The power of a convex lens of focal length 40 cm is:', o: ['−2.5 D', '+0.025 D', '+40 D', '+2.5 D'], a: 3, e: 'P = 1/f (m).', sol: ['f = 0.40 m', 'P = 1/0.40', '= 2.5', 'Answer: +2.5 D'] },
    { id: 'q56', p: ['pw1'], type: 'num', lvl: 1, q: 'A lens has power −4.0 D. Its focal length is:', o: ['−25 cm', '+25 cm', '−4 cm', '−40 cm'], a: 0, e: 'f = 1/P.', sol: ['f = 1/P', '= 1/(−4)', '= −0.25 m', 'Answer: −25 cm (concave)'] },
    { id: 'q57', p: ['pw1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Power of a diverging lens is positive.', '1 dioptre is the power of a lens of focal length 1 m.'], a: 3, e: 'Negative.' },
    { id: 'q58', p: ['e907'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'A double convex lens has R₁ = 10 cm, R₂ = −15 cm and f = 12 cm. Its refractive index is:', o: ['1.33', '1.5', '1.6', '1.4'], a: 1, e: 'Example 9.7(ii).', sol: ['1/12 = (n − 1)(1/10 + 1/15)', '= (n − 1)(1/6)', 'n − 1 = 0.5', 'Answer: n = 1.5'] },
    { id: 'q59', p: ['e907'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'A convex lens (n = 1.5) has focal length 20 cm in air. In water (n = 1.33) its focal length is about:', o: ['20 cm', '26.7 cm', '78 cm', '40 cm'], a: 2, e: 'Example 9.7(iii).', sol: ['In air: 1/20 = 0.5 K', 'In water: 1/f = (1.5/1.33 − 1)K = 0.128 K', 'f = 20 × 0.5/0.128', 'Answer: ≈ 78 cm'] },
    { id: 'q60', p: ['cl1', 'xe1'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'A convex lens of focal length 30 cm is in contact with a concave lens of focal length 20 cm. The combination has focal length:', o: ['+60 cm', '−12 cm', '+12 cm', '−60 cm (diverging)'], a: 3, e: 'Exercise 9.10.', sol: ['1/f = 1/30 − 1/20', '= (2 − 3)/60', '= −1/60', 'Answer: −60 cm, diverging'] },
    { id: 'q61', p: ['cl1'], type: 'num', lvl: 1, q: 'Lenses of power +3 D and −1 D are in contact. The combined power is:', o: ['+2 D', '+4 D', '−3 D', '+3 D'], a: 0, e: 'P = P₁ + P₂.', sol: ['P = P₁ + P₂', '= 3 − 1', '= 2', 'Answer: +2 D (f = 50 cm)'] },
    { id: 'q62', p: ['cl1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['For lenses in contact, total magnification is the product of individual magnifications.', 'For lenses in contact, focal lengths add directly.'], a: 2, e: 'Reciprocals add.' },
    { id: 'q63', p: ['e908'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 300 170" width="300" height="170" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><line x1="10" y1="90" x2="290" y2="90" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><path d="M 150 15 Q 175 90 150 165 Q 125 90 150 15" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2"/><circle cx="90" cy="90" r="3" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="90" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">F</text><circle cx="210" cy="90" r="3" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="210" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">F′</text><line x1="40" y1="90" x2="40" y2="50" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.4"/><line x1="40" y1="50" x2="150" y2="50" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="150,50 145.5,52.2 145.5,47.8" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="150" y1="50" x2="280" y2="147" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="40" y1="50" x2="280" y2="130" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="40" y="40" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">object</text><text x="240" y="160" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">real inverted image</text></svg>', q: 'In Example 9.8, the first lens (f = 10 cm) has an object at 30 cm. Its image is at:', o: ['7.5 cm', '15 cm', '20 cm', '30 cm'], a: 1, e: 'Example 9.8.', sol: ['1/v₁ − 1/(−30) = 1/10', '1/v₁ = 1/10 − 1/30 = 1/15', 'v₁ = 15 cm', 'Answer: 15 cm'] },
    { id: 'q64', p: ['e908'], type: 'st', lvl: 3, st: ['In Example 9.8, the image from the second lens is at infinity.', 'The final image is 30 cm to the right of the third lens.'], a: 0, e: 'Example 9.8.' },
    { id: 'q65', p: ['pr1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 300 180" width="300" height="180" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><polyline points="150,15 60,165 240,165 150,15" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linejoin="round"/><line x1="10" y1="120" x2="100" y2="100" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="100,100 95.3,103.7 94.2,98.6" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="100" y1="100" x2="200" y2="100" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2"/><line x1="200" y1="100" x2="290" y2="125" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="290,125 284.1,126.1 285.5,121" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="100" y1="100" x2="290" y2="58" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="250" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">δ</text><text x="150" y="40" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">A</text><text x="150" y="178" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">inside ray ∥ base at D_m</text></svg>', q: 'For a prism, r₁ + r₂ equals:', o: ['δ', 'A', '2A', 'i + e'], a: 1, e: 'Eq. 9.34.' },
    { id: 'q66', p: ['pr1'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'For a prism with A = 60°, i = 50° and e = 40°, the deviation is:', o: ['50°', '150°', '30°', '10°'], a: 2, e: 'δ = i + e − A.', sol: ['δ = i + e − A', '= 50 + 40 − 60', '= 30', 'Answer: 30°'] },
    { id: 'q67', p: ['pr2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, tag: '📈 Graph', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 270 180" width="270" height="180" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><line x1="38" y1="154" x2="266" y2="154" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="266,154 259.7,157 259.7,151" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="40" y1="156" x2="40" y2="10" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="40,10 43,16.3 37,16.3" fill="currentColor"/><text x="264" y="148" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">i (°)</text><text x="46" y="18" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">δ (°)</text><line x1="143" y1="151" x2="143" y2="157" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="143" y="168" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">i = e</text><line x1="37" y1="120.4" x2="43" y2="120.4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="34" y="124.4" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">D_m</text><polyline points="68.8,84.8 71.1,86.9 73.3,88.9 75.6,90.9 77.8,92.9 80.1,94.7 82.3,96.6 84.6,98.3 86.8,100 89.1,101.6 91.3,103.1 93.6,104.6 95.8,106 98.1,107.3 100.3,108.6 102.6,109.8 104.8,111 107.1,112 109.3,113.1 111.6,114 113.8,114.9 116.1,115.7 118.3,116.5 120.6,117.1 122.8,117.8 125.1,118.3 127.3,118.8 129.6,119.2 131.8,119.6 134.1,119.9 136.3,120.1 138.6,120.3 140.8,120.4 143.1,120.4 145.3,120.4 147.6,120.3 149.8,120.1 152.1,119.9 154.3,119.6 156.6,119.2 158.8,118.8 161.1,118.3 163.3,117.7 165.6,117.1 167.8,116.4 170,115.6 172.3,114.8 174.6,113.9 176.8,113 179.1,112 181.3,110.9 183.5,109.7 185.8,108.5 188.1,107.2 190.3,105.9 192.6,104.5 194.8,103 197,101.4 199.3,99.8 201.6,98.2 203.8,96.4 206.1,94.6 208.3,92.7 210.6,90.8 212.8,88.8 215.1,86.7 217.3,84.6 219.6,82.4 221.8,80.1 224.1,77.8 226.3,75.4 228.6,72.9 230.8,70.4 233.1,67.8 235.3,65.1 237.6,62.4 239.8,59.6 242.1,56.8 244.3,53.8 246.6,50.8 248.8,47.8" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><line x1="143" y1="154" x2="143" y2="120.4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/></svg>', q: 'At minimum deviation, the ray inside the prism is:', o: ['Perpendicular to the base', 'Along the face AB', 'Parallel to the base', 'Totally reflected'], a: 2, e: '9.6.' },
    { id: 'q68', p: ['pr2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, tag: '📈 Graph', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 270 180" width="270" height="180" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><line x1="38" y1="154" x2="266" y2="154" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="266,154 259.7,157 259.7,151" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="40" y1="156" x2="40" y2="10" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="40,10 43,16.3 37,16.3" fill="currentColor"/><text x="264" y="148" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">i (°)</text><text x="46" y="18" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">δ (°)</text><line x1="143" y1="151" x2="143" y2="157" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="143" y="168" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">i = e</text><line x1="37" y1="120.4" x2="43" y2="120.4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="34" y="124.4" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">D_m</text><polyline points="68.8,84.8 71.1,86.9 73.3,88.9 75.6,90.9 77.8,92.9 80.1,94.7 82.3,96.6 84.6,98.3 86.8,100 89.1,101.6 91.3,103.1 93.6,104.6 95.8,106 98.1,107.3 100.3,108.6 102.6,109.8 104.8,111 107.1,112 109.3,113.1 111.6,114 113.8,114.9 116.1,115.7 118.3,116.5 120.6,117.1 122.8,117.8 125.1,118.3 127.3,118.8 129.6,119.2 131.8,119.6 134.1,119.9 136.3,120.1 138.6,120.3 140.8,120.4 143.1,120.4 145.3,120.4 147.6,120.3 149.8,120.1 152.1,119.9 154.3,119.6 156.6,119.2 158.8,118.8 161.1,118.3 163.3,117.7 165.6,117.1 167.8,116.4 170,115.6 172.3,114.8 174.6,113.9 176.8,113 179.1,112 181.3,110.9 183.5,109.7 185.8,108.5 188.1,107.2 190.3,105.9 192.6,104.5 194.8,103 197,101.4 199.3,99.8 201.6,98.2 203.8,96.4 206.1,94.6 208.3,92.7 210.6,90.8 212.8,88.8 215.1,86.7 217.3,84.6 219.6,82.4 221.8,80.1 224.1,77.8 226.3,75.4 228.6,72.9 230.8,70.4 233.1,67.8 235.3,65.1 237.6,62.4 239.8,59.6 242.1,56.8 244.3,53.8 246.6,50.8 248.8,47.8" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><line x1="143" y1="154" x2="143" y2="120.4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/></svg>', ar: { A: 'Any deviation other than minimum corresponds to two angles of incidence.', R: 'i and e are interchangeable in δ = i + e − A (reversibility).' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q69', p: ['pr3', 'xe1'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'A prism of angle 60° has minimum deviation 40°. Its refractive index is about:', o: ['1.41', '1.73', '1.33', '1.53'], a: 3, e: 'Exercise 9.6.', sol: ['n = sin[(A + D_m)/2]/sin(A/2)', '= sin 50°/sin 30°', '= 0.766/0.5', 'Answer: ≈ 1.53'] },
    { id: 'q70', p: ['pr3'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'A thin prism of angle 5° has n = 1.5. Its deviation is:', o: ['2.5°', '7.5°', '5°', '1.5°'], a: 0, e: 'D = (n − 1)A.', sol: ['D = (n − 1)A', '= 0.5 × 5°', '= 2.5°', 'Answer: 2.5°'] },
    { id: 'q71', p: ['pr3', 'xe1'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'The prism (n = 1.532, A = 60°) is placed in water (n = 1.33). The new minimum deviation is about:', o: ['40°', '10°', '20°', '30°'], a: 1, e: 'Exercise 9.6.', sol: ['n_rel = 1.532/1.33 = 1.152', 'sin[(60 + D)/2] = 1.152 × 0.5 = 0.576', '(60 + D)/2 = 35.2°', 'Answer: D ≈ 10.3°'] },
    { id: 'q72', p: ['pr3', 'xe3'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'A 60° prism (n = 1.524). For the ray to just suffer TIR at the second face, angle of incidence on the first face is about:', o: ['41°', '19°', '29.75°', '60°'], a: 2, e: 'Exercise 9.21.', sol: ['i_c = sin⁻¹(1/1.524) = 41°', 'r₂ = 41° ⇒ r₁ = 19°', 'sin i = 1.524 sin 19° = 0.496', 'Answer: ≈ 29.75°'] },
    { id: 'q73', p: ['oi1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Which is NOT an optical instrument?', o: ['Periscope', 'Kaleidoscope', 'Binoculars', 'Galvanometer'], a: 3, e: '9.7.' },
    { id: 'q74', p: ['sm1'], type: 'num', lvl: 1, q: 'A simple microscope of focal length 5 cm forms the image at the near point (25 cm). Its magnification is:', o: ['5', '25', '4', '6'], a: 3, e: 'm = 1 + D/f.', sol: ['m = 1 + D/f', '= 1 + 25/5', '= 6', 'Answer: 6'] },
    { id: 'q75', p: ['sm1'], type: 'st', lvl: 1, st: ['The object is placed beyond 2f in a simple microscope.', 'A simple microscope gives an erect, magnified, virtual image.'], a: 3, e: 'Inside f.' },
    { id: 'q76', p: ['sm2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'With the image at infinity, a lens of focal length 5 cm has magnifying power:', o: ['5', '6', '25', '0.2'], a: 0, e: 'm = D/f.', sol: ['m = D/f', '= 25/5', '= 5', 'Answer: 5'] },
    { id: 'q77', p: ['sm2', 'pp2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 3, ar: { A: 'A simple microscope provides angular magnification.', R: 'It allows the object to be kept much closer than 25 cm while the image is at 25 cm.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q78', p: ['sm2', 'xe3'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'A magnifier (f = 9 cm) should be held at what distance from a card to see it with maximum magnifying power (image at 25 cm)?', o: ['9 cm', '6.6 cm', '25 cm', '4.5 cm'], a: 1, e: 'Exercise 9.23.', sol: ['v = −25 cm, f = 9 cm', '1/u = 1/v − 1/f = −1/25 − 1/9', '= −34/225', 'Answer: u ≈ −6.6 cm (m ≈ 3.8)'] },
    { id: 'q79', p: ['cm1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 320 145" width="320" height="145" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><line x1="10" y1="70" x2="310" y2="70" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><path d="M 70 30 Q 82 70 70 110 Q 58 70 70 30" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2"/><text x="70" y="125" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">objective (f₀)</text><path d="M 240 20 Q 256 70 240 120 Q 224 70 240 20" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2"/><text x="240" y="132" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">eyepiece (fₑ)</text><line x1="40" y1="70" x2="40" y2="58" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.4"/><line x1="180" y1="70" x2="180" y2="100" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.4"/><text x="180" y="112" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">real image</text><line x1="100" y1="60" x2="165" y2="60" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="165,60 160.5,62.2 160.5,57.8" fill="currentColor"/><text x="132" y="54" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">L (tube length)</text></svg>', q: 'In a compound microscope, the objective forms an image that is:', o: ['Real, inverted and magnified', 'Virtual and erect', 'Real and diminished', 'Virtual and inverted'], a: 0, e: '9.7.1.' },
    { id: 'q80', p: ['cm1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['The tube length is the distance between the second focal point of the objective and the first focal point of the eyepiece.', 'The eyepiece of a compound microscope forms a real image.'], a: 2, e: 'Final image is virtual.' },
    { id: 'q81', p: ['cm2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'A compound microscope has f₀ = 1.0 cm, f_e = 2.0 cm and tube length 20 cm. Its magnifying power (image at infinity) is:', o: ['100', '500', '250', '40'], a: 2, e: 'm = (L/f₀)(D/f_e).', sol: ['m = (L/f₀)(D/f_e)', '= (20/1)(25/2)', '= 20 × 12.5', 'Answer: 250'] },
    { id: 'q82', p: ['cm2', 'xe2'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'Objective 8.0 mm, eyepiece 2.5 cm; object at 9.0 mm from objective; final image at 25 cm. The magnifying power is:', o: ['80', '11', '100', '88'], a: 3, e: 'Exercise 9.12.', sol: ['1/v₀ = 1/0.8 − 1/0.9 ⇒ v₀ = 7.2 cm', 'm₀ = 7.2/0.9 = 8', 'm_e = 1 + 25/2.5 = 11', 'Answer: m = 88 (separation 9.47 cm)'] },
    { id: 'q83', p: ['cm2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Both objective and eyepiece of a compound microscope have short focal lengths.', R: 'Magnifying power is inversely proportional to f₀ and f_e.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q84', p: ['ts1', 'xe2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 320 120" width="320" height="120" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><line x1="10" y1="60" x2="310" y2="60" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><path d="M 60 15 Q 76 60 60 105 Q 44 60 60 15" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2"/><text x="60" y="116" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">objective (large f₀)</text><path d="M 250 35 Q 258 60 250 85 Q 242 60 250 35" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2"/><text x="255" y="100" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">eyepiece</text><line x1="60" y1="60" x2="230" y2="60" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="230,60 225.5,62.2 225.5,57.8" fill="currentColor"/><text x="140" y="52" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">f₀ + fₑ</text><line x1="10" y1="30" x2="60" y2="30" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="10" y1="40" x2="60" y2="40" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><text x="12" y="22" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">parallel rays</text></svg>', q: 'A telescope has objective 144 cm and eyepiece 6.0 cm. Its magnifying power (normal adjustment) is:', o: ['24', '150', '138', '864'], a: 0, e: 'Exercise 9.13.', sol: ['m = f₀/f_e', '= 144/6', '= 24', 'Answer: 24 (tube length 150 cm)'] },
    { id: 'q85', p: ['ts1', 'xe3'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'A telescope (f₀ = 140 cm, f_e = 5.0 cm) with final image at 25 cm has magnifying power:', o: ['28', '33.6', '145', '23.3'], a: 1, e: 'Exercise 9.27(b).', sol: ['m = (f₀/f_e)(1 + f_e/D)', '= 28 × (1 + 5/25)', '= 28 × 1.2', 'Answer: 33.6'] },
    { id: 'q86', p: ['ts1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['In normal adjustment, the length of a telescope tube is f₀ + f_e.', 'Terrestrial telescopes have extra inverting lenses to give an erect image.'], a: 0, e: '9.7.2.' },
    { id: 'q87', p: ['ts2', 'xe2'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'A telescope with f₀ = 15 m views the moon (diameter 3.48 × 10⁶ m, distance 3.8 × 10⁸ m). The diameter of the moon\'s image by the objective is about:', o: ['1.37 cm', '137 cm', '13.7 cm', '0.9 cm'], a: 2, e: 'Exercise 9.14(b).', sol: ['α = 3.48 × 10⁶/3.8 × 10⁸ = 9.16 × 10⁻³ rad', 'd = f₀α', '= 15 × 9.16 × 10⁻³ m', 'Answer: ≈ 13.7 cm'] },
    { id: 'q88', p: ['ts2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Astronomical telescopes have objectives of large diameter.', R: 'Light-gathering power and resolving power both increase with objective diameter.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q89', p: ['ts3', 'ts4'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Modern large telescopes use mirror objectives.', R: 'Mirrors have no chromatic aberration and are easier to support.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q90', p: ['ts3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'The largest lens objective in use (about 1.02 m) is at:', o: ['Kavalur, India', 'Yerkes Observatory, USA', 'Mt. Palomar, USA', 'Keck, Hawaii'], a: 1, e: '9.7.2.' },
    { id: 'q91', p: ['ts4'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 320 120" width="320" height="120" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><path d="M 50 15 Q 30 60 50 105" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2.4"/><rect x="46" y="55" width="8" height="10" fill="#fff" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><path d="M 200 45 Q 210 60 200 75" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2"/><line x1="300" y1="25" x2="60" y2="25" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="60,25 64.5,22.8 64.5,27.2" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="300" y1="95" x2="60" y2="95" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="60,95 64.5,92.8 64.5,97.2" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="60" y1="25" x2="197" y2="52" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="60" y1="95" x2="197" y2="68" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="197" y1="52" x2="40" y2="60" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="197" y1="68" x2="40" y2="60" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><text x="8" y="116" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">primary (concave) with hole</text><text x="210" y="92" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">secondary (convex)</text></svg>', q: 'In a Cassegrain telescope, the secondary mirror is:', o: ['Concave', 'Plane', 'Convex', 'Parabolic concave'], a: 2, e: '9.7.2.' },
    { id: 'q92', p: ['ts4', 'xe3'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'A Cassegrain telescope has primary R = 220 mm, secondary R = 140 mm, 20 mm apart. The final image of a distant object is formed from the secondary at:', o: ['110 mm', '90 mm', '70 mm', '315 mm'], a: 3, e: 'Exercise 9.29.', sol: ['Primary focus at 110 mm', 'Virtual object for secondary at 90 mm, f = 70 mm (convex)', '1/v = 1/70 − 1/90 = 2/630', 'Answer: v = 315 mm'] },
    { id: 'q93', p: ['ts5'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The largest telescope in India (2.34 m) is at:', o: ['Mt. Abu, Rajasthan', 'Nainital, Uttarakhand', 'Pune, Maharashtra', 'Kavalur, Tamil Nadu'], a: 3, e: '9.7.2.' },
    { id: 'q94', p: ['ts5'], type: 'st', lvl: 1, st: ['The Kavalur telescope is a refracting telescope.', 'The Keck telescopes in Hawaii have 10 m reflectors.'], a: 3, e: 'It is a Cassegrain reflector.' },
    { id: 'q95', p: ['su2'], type: 'match', lvl: 2, q: 'Match the instrument with its magnifying power.', match: { h: ['Instrument', 'Magnifying power'], l: ['Simple microscope (near point)', 'Simple microscope (infinity)', 'Compound microscope', 'Telescope (normal)'], r: ['1 + D/f', 'D/f', '(L/f₀)(D/f_e)', 'f₀/f_e'] }, o: ['A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV', 'A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV', 'A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV', 'A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I'], a: 1, e: 'Summary.' },
    { id: 'q96', p: ['su1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Mirage is an example of total internal reflection.', 'f is positive for a concave mirror.'], a: 2, e: 'Negative for concave.' },
    { id: 'q97', p: ['su2'], type: 'stmts', lvl: 2, q: 'Which formulae are correct?', s: ['1/v − 1/u = 1/f (thin lens)', 'P = 1/f', 'P = P₁ + P₂ (lenses in contact)', '1/v − 1/u = 1/f (mirror)'], o: ['I and II only', 'I, II and III only', 'II and IV only', 'I, II, III and IV'], a: 1, e: 'Mirror: 1/v + 1/u = 1/f.' },
    { id: 'q98', p: ['pp1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'A real image exists in space even without a screen.', R: 'Rays from an object point actually converge at the image point.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q99', p: ['pp1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Laws of reflection hold only for plane surfaces.', 'A page of a book does not form our image because reflection from it is irregular.'], a: 3, e: 'For all surfaces.' },
    { id: 'q100', p: ['pp2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Thick lenses give coloured images.', R: 'Different colours are refracted by different amounts (dispersion).' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q101', p: ['xe2', 'ln1'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'An object and screen are 90 cm apart; two lens positions 20 cm apart give sharp images. The focal length is:', o: ['21.4 cm', '22.5 cm', '20 cm', '35 cm'], a: 0, e: 'Exercise 9.19.', sol: ['f = (D² − d²)/4D', '= (8100 − 400)/360', '= 7700/360', 'Answer: ≈ 21.4 cm'] },
    { id: 'q102', p: ['xe2', 'ln1'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'To image a bulb on a wall 3 m away with a convex lens, the maximum possible focal length is:', o: ['1.5 m', '0.75 m', '3 m', '0.5 m'], a: 1, e: 'Exercise 9.18.', sol: ['Real image needs D ≥ 4f', 'f_max = D/4', '= 3/4', 'Answer: 0.75 m'] },
    { id: 'q103', p: ['xe3', 'rf1'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'A galvanometer mirror turns by 3.5°. The reflected spot on a screen 1.5 m away moves by about:', o: ['9.2 cm', '36.8 cm', '18.4 cm', '5.3 cm'], a: 2, e: 'Exercise 9.30.', sol: ['Reflected ray turns by 2 × 3.5° = 7°', 'd = 1.5 tan 7°', '= 1.5 × 0.1228', 'Answer: ≈ 18.4 cm'] },
    { id: 'q104', p: ['xe3', 'cm2'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'For 30X with f₀ = 1.25 cm and f_e = 5 cm (final image at 25 cm), the object should be at a distance from the objective of:', o: ['1.25 cm', '2.5 cm', '7.5 cm', '1.5 cm'], a: 3, e: 'Exercise 9.26.', sol: ['m_e = 1 + 25/5 = 6 ⇒ m₀ = 5', 'v₀ = 5|u₀|; 1/v₀ + 1/|u₀| = 1/1.25', '6/(5|u₀|) = 0.8', 'Answer: |u₀| = 1.5 cm'] },
    { id: 'q105', p: ['xe3', 'cl1'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'An equiconvex lens (n = 1.5) alone gives f = 30 cm; with a liquid layer below it the combination has f = 45 cm. The refractive index of the liquid is about:', o: ['1.33', '1.5', '1.25', '1.6'], a: 0, e: 'Exercise 9.31.', sol: ['R = 30 cm from 1/30 = 0.5 × 2/R', '1/f_liq = 1/45 − 1/30 = −1/90', '(n − 1)(1/30) = 1/90', 'Answer: n ≈ 1.33'] },
    { id: 'q106', p: ['ti1', 'rr2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'TIR can occur when light goes from air into glass.', R: 'TIR requires light to go from a denser to a rarer medium.' }, a: 3, e: 'A false, R true.' },
    { id: 'q107', p: ['fm1', 'me1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'A convex mirror has a positive focal length.', R: 'Its focus lies behind the mirror, along the direction of incident light.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q108', p: ['pw1', 'cl1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Power of a combination of lenses in contact is the sum of individual powers.', R: 'Focal lengths of lenses in contact add directly.' }, a: 2, e: 'Reciprocals add; R false.' },
    { id: 'q109', p: ['me3', 'e903'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'A concave mirror can form a virtual image.', R: 'A concave mirror always forms real images.' }, a: 2, e: 'R false.' },
    { id: 'q110', p: ['ln3', 'ln2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'A concave lens can form a real image of a real object.', R: 'A concave lens diverges light.' }, a: 3, e: 'A false, R true.' },
    { id: 'q111', p: ['pr3', 'pr2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'A thin prism produces a large deviation.', R: 'For a thin prism D = (n − 1)A.' }, a: 3, e: 'Thin prisms deviate little; A false.' },
    { id: 'q112', p: ['ts1', 'cm1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'The objective of a telescope has a small focal length.', R: 'Magnifying power of a telescope is f₀/f_e.' }, a: 3, e: 'Large f₀; A false, R true.' },
    { id: 'q113', p: ['ts4', 'ts5'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Keck telescopes are reflecting telescopes.', R: 'The largest Indian telescope is at Kavalur.' }, a: 1, e: 'Both true; unrelated.' },
    { id: 'q114', p: ['rr4', 'ti2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Apparent depth of water is less than real depth.', R: 'Diamond has a critical angle of 24.4°.' }, a: 1, e: 'Both true; R unrelated.' },
    { id: 'q115', p: ['sc1', 'me2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Magnification of a virtual image formed by a mirror is positive.', R: 'Heights measured upwards are positive in the sign convention.' }, a: 1, e: 'Both true; R alone does not explain A.' },
    { id: 'q116', p: ['ti5', 'ti4'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Cladding of an optical fibre has higher refractive index than the core.', 'Totally reflecting prisms need critical angle greater than 45°.'], a: 1, e: 'Both false.' },
    { id: 'q117', p: ['pw1', 'ln2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['A lens of power +2 D has focal length −50 cm.', 'A convex lens has negative focal length.'], a: 1, e: 'Both false.' },
    { id: 'q118', p: ['sm2', 'ts1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Magnifying power of a telescope is f_e/f₀.', 'A simple microscope with image at infinity has m = 1 + D/f.'], a: 1, e: 'Both false.' },
    { id: 'q119', p: ['in2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Ray optics is valid because the wavelength of light is:', o: ['Very small compared to the size of ordinary objects', 'Very large', 'Equal to the size of objects', 'Zero'], a: 0, e: '9.1.' },
    { id: 'q120', p: ['e902'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'For a phone along the axis of a concave mirror, the part lying in the plane through C perpendicular to the axis forms an image that is:', o: ['Twice as large', 'Of the same size, in the same plane', 'At infinity', 'At the focus'], a: 1, e: 'Example 9.2.' },
    { id: 'q121', p: ['rr3'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 260 160" width="260" height="160" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><rect x="30" y="55" width="200" height="50" fill="#D9A520" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" fill-opacity="0.15"/><line x1="60" y1="10" x2="100" y2="55" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="100,55 94.5,52.7 98.4,49.2" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="100" y1="55" x2="130" y2="105" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2"/><line x1="130" y1="105" x2="170" y2="150" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="170,150 164.5,147.7 168.4,144.2" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="100" y1="55" x2="140" y2="100" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/><line x1="140" y1="100" x2="165" y2="128" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/><text x="200" y="140" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">lateral shift</text></svg>', st: ['For a rectangular slab, the angle of emergence equals the angle of incidence.', 'Lateral shift is zero for oblique incidence on a slab.'], a: 2, e: 'It is non-zero for oblique incidence.' },
    { id: 'q122', p: ['ti3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'In the laser demonstration (Fig 9.12), when the beam strikes the water surface very obliquely from inside water:', o: ['It refracts into air completely', 'It is absorbed', 'It is totally reflected back into water', 'It travels along the surface always'], a: 2, e: '9.4.' },
    { id: 'q123', p: ['ti6'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'The main requirement in fabricating optical fibres is:', o: ['High electrical conductivity', 'Large diameter', 'Coloured glass', 'Very little absorption of light over long distances'], a: 3, e: '9.4.1.' },
    { id: 'q124', p: ['ss1', 'ln1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'The lensmaker\'s formula is derived by applying refraction at two spherical surfaces.', R: 'The image by the first surface acts as the object for the second.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q125', p: ['ss1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['The relation n₂/v − n₁/u = (n₂ − n₁)/R applies only to convex surfaces.', 'It is derived for paraxial rays.'], a: 3, e: 'Applies to any spherical surface.' },
    { id: 'q126', p: ['e905'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['In Example 9.5 the image is formed inside the glass.', 'The image in Example 9.5 is virtual.'], a: 2, e: 'It is real (rays converge in glass).' },
    { id: 'q127', p: ['oi1'], type: 'st', lvl: 1, st: ['Periscopes and kaleidoscopes use reflection.', 'The human eye is an optical device.'], a: 0, e: '9.7.' },
    { id: 'q128', p: ['su1', 'ti1'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'The critical angle of water (n = 1.33) is about:', o: ['41.1°', '48.75°', '24.4°', '37.3°'], a: 1, e: 'sin i_c = 1/n.', sol: ['sin i_c = 1/1.33', '= 0.752', 'i_c = sin⁻¹ 0.752', 'Answer: ≈ 48.75°'] },
    { id: 'q129', p: ['su1', 'ti5'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Optical fibres are coated with a material of lower refractive index.', R: 'This allows total internal reflection at the core boundary.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q130', p: ['ti2'], type: 'match', lvl: 2, q: 'Match the substance with its critical angle (Table 9.1).', match: { h: ['Substance', 'Critical angle'], l: ['Water', 'Crown glass', 'Dense flint glass', 'Diamond'], r: ['48.75°', '41.14°', '37.31°', '24.41°'] }, o: ['A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV', 'A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV', 'A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV', 'A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I'], a: 2, e: 'Table 9.1.' },
    { id: 'q131', p: ['me3', 'ln3'], type: 'match', lvl: 3, q: 'Match the optical element (real object) with its image.', match: { h: ['Element', 'Image'], l: ['Convex mirror', 'Concave lens', 'Concave mirror, object inside F', 'Convex lens, object beyond 2F'], r: ['Virtual, erect, diminished (between P and F)', 'Virtual, erect, diminished (lens)', 'Virtual, erect, magnified', 'Real, inverted, diminished'] }, o: ['A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV', 'A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV', 'A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I', 'A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV'], a: 3, e: '9.2, 9.5.' },
    { id: 'q132', p: ['ts2', 'ts3', 'ts4'], type: 'stmts', lvl: 2, q: 'Advantages of reflecting over refracting telescopes:', s: ['No chromatic aberration', 'Lighter for same optical quality', 'Can be supported over entire back surface', 'Image always erect without extra lenses'], o: ['I and II only', 'II and IV only', 'I, II and III only', 'I, II, III and IV'], a: 2, e: '9.7.2.' }
  ]
});
